## 1. ManagedServiceIdentity.Serialization.cs

File: `Common/Custom/Models/ManagedServiceIdentity.Serialization.cs`

### Line 31: `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType, jOptions);`

The `ManagedServiceIdentityType` has a custom JsonConverter (`ManagedServiceIdentityTypeConverter`) whose `Write` method does:
```csharp
writer.WritePropertyName("type");
writer.WriteStringValue(model.ToString());
```

So replace `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType, jOptions);` with:
```csharp
writer.WritePropertyName("type"u8);
writer.WriteStringValue(ManagedServiceIdentityType.ToString());
```

### Line 49: `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, item.Value, jOptions);`

`item.Value` is of type `UserAssignedIdentity` which implements `IJsonModel<UserAssignedIdentity>`. Replace with:
```csharp
writer.WriteObjectValue(item.Value, ModelReaderWriterOptions.Json);
```

Ensure `using System.ClientModel.Primitives;` is in the file (it should already be there).

### Line 206: `type = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ManagedServiceIdentityType>($"{{{property}}}");`

The `ManagedServiceIdentityTypeConverter.Read` method parses a JSON object `{"type": "value"}` and extracts the string from the first property's value. Since `property` is a `JsonProperty` with name "type" and value being the string, we can inline this as:
```csharp
type = new ManagedServiceIdentityType(property.Value.GetString());
```

### Line 218: `dictionary.Add(new ResourceIdentifier(property0.Name), JsonSerializer.Deserialize<UserAssignedIdentity>(property0.Value.GetRawText()));`

